<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hispania Ulterior</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">197 BCE–1st century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a> · Also known as Further Spain</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispania">Hispania</a> Ulterior ('Further <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>') was a Roman province created in 197 BCE in the south and west of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberian Peninsula</a>, centred on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guadalquivir">Baetis</a> (Guadalquivir) valley, with Corduba as its main city. Under Augustus it was divided into the provinces of Baetica and Lusitania. Its rich mines and farmland made it one of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>'s most valuable western territories.</p></div>
